  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "title": "The six plain vowels",
      "titleKo": "기본 모음",
      "groups": [
        "vowels-basic"
      ],
      "intro": [
        "Korean is written in square blocks, one block per syllable. Each block is built from letters, and there are only about forty of them.",
        "Start with the vowels, because every block has one. Six of them are enough to read a surprising amount."
      ],
      "rules": [
        "Every block starts with a consonant. When the sound starts with a vowel, the letter ㅇ holds the empty seat and says nothing: 아 is just a.",
        "A tall vowel (ㅏ ㅓ ㅣ) sits to the right of the consonant. A wide vowel (ㅗ ㅜ ㅡ) sits underneath it."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "ko": "아",
          "note": "ㅇ + ㅏ. The ㅇ is silent."
        },
        {
          "ko": "오",
          "note": "ㅇ + ㅗ. A wide vowel goes underneath."
        },
        {
          "ko": "이",
          "note": "ㅇ + ㅣ"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "title": "The ten plain consonants",
      "titleKo": "기본 자음",
      "groups": [
        "cons-plain"
      ],
      "intro": [
        "Each consonant is a drawing of the mouth making it: ㄱ is the back of the tongue lifting, ㄴ is the tip touching behind the teeth, ㅁ is the closed lips.",
        "Put one in front of a vowel and you have a syllable. With these ten and the six vowels you can already read most of the blocks on a sign."
      ],
      "rules": [
        "ㄱ ㄷ ㅂ sound softer at the start of a word (g, d, b) and harder at the end of a block (k, t, p). Korean ears hear them as one sound.",
        "ㄹ is one quick tap of the tongue — between r and l. Before a vowel it leans r, at the bottom of a block it leans l."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "ko": "가",
          "note": "ㄱ + ㅏ"
        },
        {
          "ko": "나",
          "note": "ㄴ + ㅏ"
        },
        {
          "ko": "고",
          "note": "ㄱ + ㅗ, the vowel underneath"
        },
        {
          "ko": "무",
          "note": "ㅁ + ㅜ"
        },
        {
          "ko": "시",
          "note": "ㅅ + ㅣ — ㅅ leans towards sh before ㅣ"
        },
        {
          "ko": "하",
          "note": "ㅎ + ㅏ"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "title": "The y vowels, and ㅐ ㅔ",
      "titleKo": "이중 모음",
      "groups": [
        "vowels-y"
      ],
      "intro": [
        "Add a second short stroke to a vowel and a y slides in front of it: ㅏ becomes ㅑ, ㅓ becomes ㅕ.",
        "ㅐ and ㅔ look different and were once said differently. Today almost nobody separates them — you tell them apart by knowing the word, not by hearing it."
      ],
      "rules": [],
      "examples": [
        {
          "ko": "야",
          "note": "ㅇ + ㅑ"
        },
        {
          "ko": "여",
          "note": "ㅇ + ㅕ"
        },
