      "intro": [
        "A consonant can sit at the bottom of a block. It is called 받침, and it closes the syllable: 바 + ㅂ = 밥.",
        "This is where reading Korean gets tricky, because the letter at the bottom is not always the sound you say."
      ],
      "rules": [
        "Any consonant can close a block, but only seven sounds ever come out: k, n, t, l, m, p, ng.",
        "ㅅ ㅆ ㅈ ㅊ ㅌ ㅎ at the bottom are all said as a stopped t: 옷 is said ot, not os. 꽃 is kkot.",
        "ㅋ ㄲ at the bottom are said k. ㅍ at the bottom is said p: 앞 is ap.",
        "When the next block starts with ㅇ, the closing sound slides across into it: 한국어 is said 한구거. That comes later, in real words — for now, read one block at a time."
